Sure, here is a PHP implementation of your code with added comments:

```PHP
function compareCharOfStrings($txt1, $txt2) {
    // Check if both strings are not empty
    if(trim($txt1) === "" || trim($txt2) === "") return false;

    // Obtain the last character of txt1 and the first character of txt2
    $lastCharOfTxt1 = substr($txt1, -1);
    $firstCharOfTxt2 = substr($txt2, 0, 1);

    // Check if they are both alphabetical (i.e. not digits or special characters)
    // and are not part of a word in their respective strings
    if(!ctype_alpha($lastCharOfTxt1)) return false;
    if(!ctype_alpha($firstCharOfTxt2)) return false;

    // Check the character before the last in first string and second character in second string
    // using the ctype_alpha() function to check if they're alphabets
    // then negate to check if they're not alphabets
    // Check if they are part of a word
    if(!ctype_alpha(substr($txt1, -2, 1))) return false;
    if(!ctype_alpha(substr($txt2, 1, 1))) return false;

    return true;
}
```

Please note that unlike JavaScript, PHP doesn't support Unicode string manipulation directly on subscripts. As a result, the `substr` function is used instead which functions similarly to the substring method in JavaScript. Also `ctype_alpha` function is used to check if characters are alphabetical, so no need to check lower or uppercase manually.

