```svelte
<script>
    let count = 0;

    function increment() {
        count += 1;
    }
</script>

<button on:click={increment}>
    Clicked {count} {count === 1 ? 'time' : 'times'}
</button>
```

In the above code, we have a simple Svelte component which contains a state variable `count` which is initially set to zero. We've also defined a method `increment` which increases the `count` variable by one.

There's a button in the template section of the component. When this button is clicked, it triggers the `increment` function. The `{count}` inside the button interpolates the value of count into the DOM. 

This is an example of a reactive statement. Each time the state of `count` variable changes as a result of clicking the button, Svelte re-runs the code that depends on it, updating the button text content.

